//! Entries slice 100 — Batch 246 (Plan 250 / Research 278): the prism fork
//! delta, slice 1 of 1 — one own `kernel_opt` rule from
//! PrismML-Eng/llama.cpp @ 71c14732072dcdb959a5a2dac250c8da2d45bd22
//! (#363, MIT, 2026-10-15; read at the vendor clone
//! `../vendor/prismml-llama.cpp` (the M3/4090 vendor twin)).
//!
//! - `shard-granularity-lcm-never-cut-rotation-block` — multi-GPU tensor
//!   split of blocked structures (category L): a shard boundary must
//!   respect EVERY block constraint on the axis (lcm of the quant type
//!   block and the rotation block); the rotation's reshape must keep the
//!   axis boundaries (4D, never the 2D fold) so the split axis survives;
//!   a view over a grouped output inherits the parent's split state
//!   adapted to its own geometry; an elementwise op over a feature-sharded
//!   activation slices its full-width mirrored operand per shard.
//!
//! League intel (not a bout): the fork now serves OUR league model Bonsai 2
//! 27B `PQ2_0/Q2_0` under 2-GPU tensor split (2x H200, mean KLD 2.5e-4 vs
//! single GPU, top-1 99.3%); single-GPU outputs bit-identical to base.

use super::KernelRule;

pub fn entries() -> Vec<KernelRule> {
    vec![
        // ── L: the shard-alignment law family — every block constraint
        // on the axis is a shard constraint; the lcm is the granularity;
        // the reshape must keep the axis boundaries alive. ──
        KernelRule {
            rule_id: "shard-granularity-lcm-never-cut-rotation-block".to_string(),
            gpu_concept: "MECHANISM (multi-GPU tensor split of blocked structures; category L — layout): a shard boundary must respect EVERY block constraint on the axis, never only the quantization one — for Hadamard-rotated weights the split granularity is the LCM of the type block and the rotation block, verbatim: 'Split blocks for Hadamard-rotated weights use lcm(type block, rotation block) so shards never cut a rotation block.' THE FOUNDING FAILURE (commit message, verbatim): 'Tensor split aborted on qwen35 models with folded Hadamard rotations at ggml-backend-meta.cpp:1086 (split_state.ne[j] % div == 0): the rotation reshape tiles the activation feature axis, and the meta backend had no split state for it.' FOUR FACES, one law: (1) THE AXIS-SURVIVAL RESHAPE, verbatim: 'llama_mul_mat_hadamard reshapes to 4D [n, ne0/n, ne1, ne2*ne3] so the split axis survives the rotation, and asserts ne0 % n == 0.' — the old 2D reshape [n, nelements/n] folded ne1 and ne2*ne3 into one axis, destroying the per-axis boundaries the splitter needs; a reshape that will be split later must keep every live axis boundary in the shape, and the divisibility it assumes is asserted, not hoped. (2) THE LCM GRANULARITY (llama-model.cpp): 'int64_t blck_size = ggml_blck_size(tc.tensor_axis_0->type); if (const auto it = ud->model->hadamard_weight_blocks.find(tc.tensor_axis_0->name); it != ud->model->hadamard_weight_blocks.end()) { blck_size = std::lcm(blck_size, int64_t(it->second)); }' — the per-tensor rotation block size rides a name-keyed map (hadamard_weight_blocks) and the granularity is DERIVED, never a magic constant; the grouped-GDN ssm_out row widens the grain by the group factor: 'granularity_qkv * (hparams.ssm_dt_rank / hparams.ssm_n_group)'. (3) VIEW SPLIT-STATE INHERITANCE (ggml-backend-meta.cpp handle_view): a view over a sub-block of a grouped output carries the parent's split adapted to the view's OWN geometry — for a GATED_DELTA_NET output view at offset 0, when v is taller than q by an integer factor, verbatim: 'ssm_out uses a value_dim split when GDN V is grouped, and views of the GATED_DELTA_NET output carry the matching axis-1 split.' — the code returns '{GGML_BACKEND_SPLIT_AXIS_1, {0}, {uint32_t(v->ne[1] / q->ne[1])}, 1}' under 'tensor->ne[0] == v->ne[0] && tensor->ne[1] == v->ne[1] && v->ne[1] > q->ne[1] && v->ne[1] % q->ne[1] == 0'. (4) MIRRORED-OPERAND SLICING (init_tensor + get_split_state): elementwise MUL/ADD/SUB/DIV of a feature-sharded activation with a full-width mirrored operand — the result inherits the shard and each shard's build re-points src1 to ITS slice, verbatim: 'Elementwise MUL/ADD/SUB/DIV of a feature-sharded activation with a full-width mirrored operand slices the operand per shard (ADD_ID is excluded).' — the in-code comment names the reason and the exception: 'feature-sharded activation times a full-width mirrored operand: each shard reads its slice of src1 (see init_tensor), which is only done for the four elementwise ops'; the slice is a ggml_view_4d at 'offset * source->nb[0]' with the shard's accumulated ne-offsets; ADD_ID keeps its own pre-existing rule above the new case. Plus the refinement the batch carries: an axis-1-to-axis-0 rewrite now preserves the repeat when it is single-segment ('ret.nr[0] = src_ss[0].n_segments == 1 ? src_ss[0].nr[0] : 1;') and a MIRRORED-x-axis>=2 matmul returns the activation's split (src_ss[1]) — the batched-matmul rule's missing symmetric case. MEASURED (source-reported, foreign boxes — provenance, never bars), verbatim: 'Tested on 2x H200: Bonsai 2 27B PQ2_0 and Q2_0 now run under -sm tensor with mean KLD 2.5e-4 vs single GPU (top-1 99.3%). Single-GPU CUDA output and Qwen3-4B / Qwen3.5-4B results (single and tensor) are bit-identical to the base commit.' ATTRIBUTION (commit message, verbatim): 'Split rules by Benjamin Brown (@KodeMunkie), posted on ggml-org/llama.cpp#29600; ADD_ID guard added here.' DECISION CONTENT: (a) a split planner's granularity is the LCM of EVERY semantic block on the axis — quant type block, rotation block, group factor — one overlooked block means shards that straddle it and must gather across device boundaries to reconstruct; (b) shapes fed to a splitter must CARRY their axis boundaries — a 2D fold of a 4D layout is information destruction downstream cannot repair (the assert ne0 % n == 0 converts the silent case into a loud one); (c) views are split-state citizens: a view's split is derived from its parent's, scaled by the view's own geometry — never re-derived from scratch and never defaulted to mirrored; (d) the cost of a wrong shard plan is not a crash but an ABORT (split_state.ne[j] % div == 0 refusal) or silent cross-device gathers — the KLD/top-1 oracle is the acceptance instrument. REGIME: tensor/model-parallel weight sharding over quantized + transformed (rotated/folded/grouped) weight layouts — any multi-device or multi-buffer split of block-coded data where two block structures coexist on one axis. ANTI-REGIME: single-device serving (no shard boundaries exist); tensors with ONE block structure on the axis (the lcm degenerates to it); splits at fixed known offsets that never land inside a block (the lcm never binds). KINSHIP (do not double-count): reshape-relabel-axis-scramble-content-oracle (entries_gpu_92, B233) owns the CONTENT oracle for a mis-declared reshape (a reshape re-labels bounds over fixed memory and cannot permute axes — compare contents, not shapes); THIS owns the SHAPE-SURVIVAL law for the splitter's benefit (keep the axis boundaries so the split state is derivable at all) — the two are the two halves of one reshape contract: keep the boundaries (this) or prove the contents when you fold them (B233); they cross-cite. smem-fill-align-chunk-not-element (N) and stride-align-vectorization-key (L) own WITHIN-kernel memory alignment for vectorization — THIS owns CROSS-device shard alignment to semantic blocks (a different axis and a different failure: correctness of the split, not width of the load). l2-grouped-tile-schedule (H) schedules tiles within one device's L2 — never a device boundary. The ragged-chunk-pad family (J) owns WHERE PADDING COMES FROM for ragged tails — THIS owns NEVER CUTTING a block in the first place (the pad family answers 'the tail is short', this answers 'the interior boundary is wrong'). quantized-stored-bytes-not-widened (B242 rider) owns packed-KV views-in-place — the view face here is the SPLIT-STATE half, not the storage half. NOVEL (grep tensor.?split|shard.{0,25}(cut|block|boundary)|lcm(|rotation block|mirrored operand|split granularity over both corpora at claim: ZERO hits — no rule owns multi-device shard alignment; the only 'shard' vocabulary is per-thread state shards (tree-verify-recurrent-state-parent-reload, B77) and the packed-KV views rider — different axes). NotBenchable here (a multi-GPU tensor-split host does not exist in this corpus's served paths — the KLD 2.5e-4 / top-1 99.3% figures are source-reported on 2x H200; the adoptable content is the lcm derivation + the axis-survival reshape + the view/operand propagation, all decision content). LANE INTEL: the fork (our league opponent lineage) can now serve OUR league model Bonsai 2 27B PQ2_0 across 2 GPUs — irrelevant to the single-4090 league posture, load-bearing for any future multi-GPU bout. Source: PrismML-Eng/llama.cpp @ 71c14732072dcdb959a5a2dac250c8da2d45bd22 (#363 'ggml : support -sm tensor for Hadamard-folded qwen35 (Bonsai 2)', MIT, 2026-10-15) — ggml/src/ggml-backend-meta.cpp (the elementwise guard + nr[0] refinement + the mirrored-x-axis>=2 case + handle_view's GATED_DELTA_NET view rule + init_tensor's per-shard ggml_view_4d slice), src/llama-impl.h (llama_mul_mat_hadamard's 4D reshape + the ne0 % n assert), src/llama-model.cpp (the hadamard_weight_blocks lcm site + the grouped-GDN ssm_out granularity row) — every quote verbatim at the pin this session. Batch 246 / Plan 250 / Research 278.".to_string(),
            ref_shape: "// ref_shape: the naive split planner aligns to the quant block ONLY --\n// a rotation block straddles the boundary, so shard k needs shard k-1's\n// tail to finish its rotation (a cross-device gather, or an outright\n// split refusal). And the rotation's reshape folded the axes to 2D, so\n// the planner cannot even find the feature-axis boundaries.\n#![allow(dead_code)]\n\n#[derive(Clone, Copy)]\nstruct Shard {\n    start: usize,\n    len: usize,\n}\n\nfn plan_shards_naive(ne0: usize, quant_block: usize, _rot_block: usize, n_devices: usize) -> Vec<Shard> {\n    let per = (ne0 + n_devices - 1) / n_devices;\n    // aligned to the quant block only -- a rotation block may straddle\n    let per = (per + quant_block - 1) / quant_block * quant_block;\n    let mut out = Vec::new();\n    let mut start = 0;\n    while start < ne0 {\n        let len = per.min(ne0 - start);\n        out.push(Shard { start, len });\n        start += len;\n    }\n    out\n}\n\n// the 2D fold: ne1 and ne2*ne3 collapsed into one axis -- the split\n// state is UNDERIVABLE from this shape (the splitter aborts)\nfn rotation_reshape_2d(ne: [usize; 4], n: usize) -> [usize; 2] {\n    let total: usize = ne.iter().product();\n    [n, total / n]\n}\n".to_string(),
            opt_pattern: "// opt_pattern: (1) granularity = lcm(quant block, rotation block) -- a\n// shard boundary NEVER cuts a rotation block; (2) the reshape keeps\n// every live axis boundary (4D [n, ne0/n, ne1, ne2*ne3]) with the\n// divisibility ASSERTED, not hoped; (3) a view over a grouped output\n// inherits the parent's split scaled by its own geometry; (4) an\n// elementwise op over a full-width mirrored operand slices that\n// operand per shard (ADD_ID excepted).\n#![allow(dead_code)]\n\nfn gcd(a: usize, b: usize) -> usize {\n    if b == 0 { a } else { gcd(b, a % b) }\n}\n\nfn lcm(a: usize, b: usize) -> usize {\n    a / gcd(a, b) * b\n}\n\n#[derive(Clone, Copy)]\nstruct Shard {\n    start: usize,\n    len: usize,\n}\n\n// the naive comparator: quant-aligned only -- the must-fail twin\nfn plan_shards_naive(ne0: usize, quant_block: usize, _rot_block: usize, n_devices: usize) -> Vec<Shard> {\n    let per = (ne0 + n_devices - 1) / n_devices;\n    let per = (per + quant_block - 1) / quant_block * quant_block;\n    let mut out = Vec::new();\n    let mut start = 0;\n    while start < ne0 {\n        let len = per.min(ne0 - start);\n        out.push(Shard { start, len });\n        start += len;\n    }\n    out\n}\n\n// the OLD fold comparator: 2D, boundaries destroyed\nfn rotation_reshape_2d(ne: [usize; 4], n: usize) -> [usize; 2] {\n    let total: usize = ne.iter().product();\n    [n, total / n]\n}\n\nfn plan_shards(ne0: usize, quant_block: usize, rot_block: usize, n_devices: usize) -> Vec<Shard> {\n    // every block constraint on the axis is a shard constraint\n    let grain = lcm(quant_block, rot_block);\n    let per = (ne0 + n_devices - 1) / n_devices;\n    let per = (per + grain - 1) / grain * grain;\n    let mut out = Vec::new();\n    let mut start = 0;\n    while start < ne0 {\n        let len = per.min(ne0 - start);\n        out.push(Shard { start, len });\n        start += len;\n    }\n    out\n}\n\n// the axis-survival reshape: 4D, boundaries kept, divisibility asserted\nfn rotation_reshape_4d(ne: [usize; 4], n: usize) -> [usize; 4] {\n    assert_eq!(ne[0] % n, 0, \"the fold assumed ne0 % n == 0\");\n    [n, ne[0] / n, ne[1], ne[2] * ne[3]]\n}\n\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, PartialEq)]\nstruct SplitState {\n    axis: u8,\n    repeat: usize,\n    segments: usize,\n}\n\n// a view over a grouped-GDN output: v is an integer multiple taller\n// than q -- the view's split state is the parent's axis-1 split,\n// repeat = g (the view geometry carries its own split)\nfn view_split_state(q_ne1: usize, v_ne1: usize, parent: SplitState) -> Option<SplitState> {\n    if parent.axis == 0 && v_ne1 > q_ne1 && v_ne1 % q_ne1 == 0 {\n        Some(SplitState { axis: 1, repeat: v_ne1 / q_ne1, segments: 1 })\n    } else {\n        None\n    }\n}\n\n// elementwise op: a feature-sharded activation against a full-width\n// MIRRORED operand -- the shard's build re-points src1 to its slice\n// (ADD_ID keeps its own rule and is excepted)\nfn mirrored_operand_slice(src1_ne0: usize, shard: &Shard) -> (usize, usize) {\n    let len = shard.len.min(src1_ne0 - shard.start);\n    (shard.start, len)\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn shard_boundaries_never_cut_a_rotation_block() {\n    // quant block 32, rotation block 24: lcm 96 -- naive per-device 64\n    // (quant-aligned) CUTS the 24-block at 64; the lcm plan never does\n    let shards = plan_shards(256, 32, 24, 4);\n    for s in &shards {\n        assert_eq!(s.start % 24, 0, \"boundary {} cuts a rotation block\", s.start);\n        assert_eq!(s.start % 32, 0, \"boundary {} cuts a quant block\", s.start);\n    }\n    assert_eq!(shards.iter().map(|s| s.len).sum::<usize>(), 256);\n    // the must-fail twin: the naive quant-only plan straddles\n    let naive = plan_shards_naive(256, 32, 24, 4);\n    assert!(naive.iter().any(|s| s.start % 24 != 0));\n\n    // (face 1) the axis-survival reshape: every live boundary kept\n    let ne = [64usize, 4, 8, 2];\n    let shaped = rotation_reshape_4d(ne, 8);\n    assert_eq!(shaped, [8, 8, 4, 16]);\n    // divisibility asserted, not hoped: the must-fail twin\n    let boom = std::panic::catch_unwind(|| rotation_reshape_4d([63, 4, 8, 2], 8));\n    assert!(boom.is_err(), \"a non-divisible fold must refuse\");\n    // the OLD 2D fold collapsed ne1 and ne2*ne3 into one axis (the\n    // split state is underivable from this shape)\n    assert_eq!(rotation_reshape_2d(ne, 8), [8, 512]);\n\n    // (face 3) the view rule: a GDN output with v 4x taller than q\n    let parent = SplitState { axis: 0, repeat: 1, segments: 1 };\n    let view = view_split_state(8, 32, parent).unwrap();\n    assert_eq!(view, SplitState { axis: 1, repeat: 4, segments: 1 });\n    assert!(view_split_state(8, 12, parent).is_none()); // non-integer g\n\n    // (face 4) the mirrored operand: each shard reads its own slice\n    let shard = Shard { start: 128, len: 64 };\n    assert_eq!(mirrored_operand_slice(256, &shard), (128, 64));\n    // the tail shard clamps at the operand's width\n    let tail = Shard { start: 224, len: 64 };\n    assert_eq!(mirrored_operand_slice(256, &tail), (224, 32));\n}\n".to_string(),
        },
    ]
}
